WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.923
Checks and balances: limit what the output can do.

2
00:00:03.203 --> 00:00:08.493
If you can’t guarantee the model will ignore injected instructions, control the consequences.

3
00:00:08.773 --> 00:00:12.635
OWASP’s mitigation list reads like a checks-and-balances charter.

4
00:00:12.915 --> 00:00:16.179
Constrain the model’s role and task in the system prompt.

5
00:00:16.459 --> 00:00:20.981
Define the expected output format and validate it with deterministic code.

6
00:00:21.261 --> 00:00:22.947
Filter inputs and outputs.

7
00:00:23.227 --> 00:00:27.835
Give the application, not the model, the credentials it needs, with least privilege.

8
00:00:28.115 --> 00:00:30.781
Require human approval for high-risk actions.

9
00:00:31.061 --> 00:00:33.685
Clearly separate and label external content.

10
00:00:33.965 --> 00:00:38.893
And test adversarially, treating the model as an untrusted user of your own system.

11
00:00:39.173 --> 00:00:45.915
Willison’s “lethal trifecta” (June 2025) gives a quick risk test for anything with tools.

12
00:00:46.195 --> 00:00:57.907
If one system combines access to private data, exposure to untrusted content, and a way to communicate externally, an attacker who controls the content can try to make it send the data out.

13
00:00:58.187 --> 00:01:01.195
Remove any one leg and that attack loses its path.

14
00:01:01.475 --> 00:01:05.251
For this studio, the practical upshot is modest but important.

15
00:01:05.531 --> 00:01:08.347
Never let model output trigger an action directly.

16
00:01:08.627 --> 00:01:16.115
Parse it, validate it against a contract (week 3), and gate side effects behind code checks or human approval (week 4).

17
00:01:16.395 --> 00:01:24.672
Include injection attempts in your test set, as the lab does, so you notice when a prompt or model change makes them succeed more often.

18
00:01:24.952 --> 00:01:27.235
Studio 5 returns to this in depth.
